  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00029` — **Concurrency Stampede** (`unbounded_async_fanout`), `test/utils.ts:38-46`, symbol `run → mkdir`, severity medium / confidence 0.88.\n\nThe two evidence lines you asked for, verbatim:\n\n> `fan-out: Promise.all at line 38`\n> `collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)`\n\nwith the per-element work and the closing line that names the mechanism:\n\n> `per-element work (2):`\n> `  filesystem operation — fsp.mkdir at line 43`\n> `  filesystem operation — fsp.writeFile at line 44`\n> `no bound visible: no `.slice()` or `take`/`limit` on the source, no batching, no concurrency-limit helper, no semaphore`\n> `if the collection holds N elements, N concurrent filesystem operation start at once — N is a property of the data, not of this code`\n\nOne correction to the finding's own scoring: it credits `+0.14 (collection comes from an awaited call (query, request, or listing))`, but `files` is the second **parameter** of `run()`, not an awaited listing. The mis-attribution doesn't change the verdict — a caller-supplied object is just as unbounded from this code's point of view — it just means the real confidence is a notch below 0.88.\n\n## Why it's load-dependent, and therefore machine-dependent\n\n`test/utils.ts:38` opens one `writeFile` per key in `files` and waits for all of them. The fan-out width is `Object.keys(files).length` — decided by whichever test called `run()`, in a plain object literal, with no edit to this helper. `run` has 9 direct importers (`blast_radius_direct_importers: 9`), so every test file in the suite gets to pick N.\n\nEMFILE is thrown when the process's open descriptors exceed `RLIMIT_NOFILE`. That's an inequality with a data-dependent left side and a machine-dependent right side:\n\n- **Left side (N).** A test with 3 fixture files peaks at ~3 concurrent descriptors. A test declaring 200 files peaks at ~200 — plus the transient handles `fsp.mkdir` takes on line 43, which run in the same unbounded wave.\n- **Right side (the limit).** macOS defaults to `ulimit -n 256` per process; Linux CI containers commonly sit at 1024, but a GitHub Actions runner is also handling the checkout, the node process, and open sockets against the same table.\n\nTwo more multipliers make it intermittent rather than deterministic:\n\n1. **Vitest's default pool is `threads`.** Worker threads share one process's file-descriptor table, so the peak that matters is the *sum* across every test file running concurrently, not any single `run()` call. Whether two 100-file fixtures overlap depends on scheduler timing, which is why the same CI job passes on a retry.\n2. **Locally you usually run one file.** `vitest run test/dts.test.ts` puts one `run()` in flight against a 256-descriptor budget with 3 fixture files in it. The inequality is never close to binding, so the bug is invisible — you are not testing the failing configuration, you are testing a much smaller one.\n\nSo \"doesn't reproduce locally\" isn't evidence against the diagnosis; it's the predicted behaviour. The code has no ceiling of its own, so the only ceiling is the OS's, and both sides of that comparison move when you change machines.\n\n## The bounded version\n\nI left `evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/` untouched — it's scanner input, and editing it would move the eval baseline. Here's the replacement for `test/utils.ts:37-46`, plus the helper:\n\n```ts\n// Cap in-flight file handles. Fixtures decide how many files they declare;\n// this helper decides how many are open at once.\nconst WRITE_CONCURRENCY = 16\n\nasync function mapWithConcurrency<T>(\n  items: T[],\n  limit: number,\n  fn: (item: T) => Promise<unknown>,\n) {\n  let cursor = 0\n  const workers = Array.from(\n    { length: Math.min(limit, items.length) },\n    async () => {\n      while (cursor < items.length) {\n        await fn(items[cursor++])\n      }\n    },\n  )\n  await Promise.all(workers)\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n  // Write entry files on disk\n  const names = Object.keys(files)\n\n  // Distinct parents first — usually a handful, and creating them up front\n  // means the write pass never races two mkdirs on the same path.\n  const parentDirs = [\n    ...new Set(\n      names.map((name) => path.dirname(path.resolve(testDir, name))),\n    ),\n  ]\n  await mapWithConcurrency(parentDirs, WRITE_CONCURRENCY, (dir) =>\n    fsp.mkdir(dir, { recursive: true }),\n  )\n\n  await mapWithConcurrency(names, WRITE_CONCURRENCY, (name) =>\n    fsp.writeFile(path.resolve(testDir, name), files[name], 'utf8'),\n  )\n```\n\nNotes on the shape:\n\n- **Worker pool, not batches.** A `for` loop over `chunk(names, 16)` would also bound the width, but it adds a barrier per chunk — every batch waits on its slowest write. The pool keeps 16 slots saturated until the list is drained, so wall-clock is unchanged from the `Promise.all` version for any realistic N.\n- **`cursor++` is safe.** There's no `await` between the read and the increment, so no two workers can claim the same index.\n- **Deduping the parents** cuts the operation count roughly in half for the common case where every fixture file lands in the same directory, and removes the redundant concurrent `mkdir` calls on identical paths that line 43's comment was papering over.\n- **16 is arbitrary but principled.** It's far below any `RLIMIT_NOFILE` you'll meet — including macOS's 256 with several vitest threads sharing it — and far above the point where fs throughput stops improving. This matches the finding's own `bound_concurrency` action: *\"A limit of 5-20 usually costs nothing in wall-clock and removes the failure mode entirely.\"*\n\nNo new dependency: `p-map` or `tinypool` would do the same job, but a test helper importing a concurrency library to write fixture files is more surface than the problem warrants.",
